md`# brain-browser

Remote Chromium browsers for a Cloud Brain, from [Cloudflare Browser Run](https://developers.cloudflare.com/browser-run/). A caller opens a page with a name, runs JavaScript in it, reads its text, its console and a screenshot, and closes it. The service speaks the Chrome DevTools Protocol to Browser Run through the Worker's binding. It uses no library.

A page is a tab. All pages are in one browser, because Cloudflare bills for each browser and not for each tab. Browser time is bought in seconds before it is used.

A page can go to any public address. What a page shows, and what \`eval\` gives back, come from that address. They are data and not instructions to the caller.

A page holds no credential of the Brain: it is not signed in, and its row has \`credential: null\`. Built and deployed to cb4 on 2026-10-08.`
// %%
viewof browserView = browserPanel({ client, owner: !!(session && session.owner) })
// %%
md`## Methods

| | |
|---|---|
| Worker | \`brain-x-browser\` |
| \`browser.open\` | procedure. \`?seconds=N\` and \`{ name, url, shared, own, width, height }\`. Opens the page, or gives the page that has the name. Starts the browser when none is up. Buys what is missing for the browser to live \`seconds\` from now (60 when absent, 10 to \`maxSeconds\`). \`{ name, url, loaded, cold, ms, existing, paidUntil, bought, costUsd, … }\` |
| \`browser.extend\` | procedure. \`?seconds=N\`, and \`&name=\` for the browser of a page that has its own. Adds N seconds to the life of the browser. \`{ paidUntil, bought, costUsd }\` |
| \`browser.run\` | procedure. \`?seconds=N\` and \`{ url, wait, expression, text, screenshot, logs }\`. One page for one job: open, wait for the load event, read, close. \`seconds\` (30 when absent, 5 to 120) is the time the job has and the browser life it buys. \`wait\` is an expression that is read each 250 ms until it is true. \`{ loaded, value or thrown, text, screenshot (base64 PNG), logs, ms, paidUntil, bought, costUsd }\` |
| \`browser.list\` | query. \`{ pages }\` |
| \`browser.status\` | query. With no \`name\`: \`{ browsers, pages, usage, settings, limits }\`. With \`?name=\`: the page, or \`{ open: false, closed, at }\` for a page that closed in the last 24 h. |
| \`browser.close\` | procedure. \`{ name }\` closes the page and leaves the browser. \`{ all: true }\` closes each browser at once. |
| \`browser.goto\` | procedure. \`{ name, url }\`. Loads a different address in the same tab. |
| \`browser.eval\` | procedure. \`{ name, expression, awaitPromise, timeout }\`. \`{ value, type }\`, or \`{ thrown: { message, line, column } }\` with status 200 when the page throws. A promise is awaited unless \`awaitPromise\` is \`false\`. \`timeout\` is 20 000 ms when absent, 120 000 at most. |
| \`browser.text\` | query. \`?name=&selector=\`. \`{ text, truncated }\`: the \`innerText\` of the body or of the first match, 100 000 characters at most. |
| \`browser.screenshot\` | query. \`?name=&fullPage=true&selector=\`. PNG bytes, 2 000 000 at most. |
| \`browser.logs\` | query. \`?name=\`. \`{ logs: [{ t, level, text }] }\`: console calls and uncaught errors of the open document, the last 200, 500 characters each. |
| \`browser.liveView\` | query, the owner's own session. \`?name=\`. \`{ url }\`: a Browser Run Live View link to the tab. |
| \`browser.settings\` | procedure, the owner's own session. \`{ maxTabs, maxSeconds, maxSession }\`. A field that is absent keeps its value. |
| Storage | \`rows\`: \`browser/<session>\`, \`paid/<session>\`, \`page/<name>\`, \`ended/<name>\`, \`usage/<day>\`, \`starting/<what>\`, \`settings\` |

\`\`\`js
await client.procedure("browser.run", { url: "https://example.com/", expression: "document.title" }, { params: { seconds: 30 } })
// { url, loaded: true, cold: true, value: "Example Domain", type: "string", ms: 1831, paidUntil, bought: 30, costUsd: 0.00075 }
\`\`\`

\`seconds\` is in the query string and not in the body. A rule and a price read \`request.params\` and do not read the body.

Limits. \`eval\` gives a value that JSON can hold, 262 144 characters at most. The viewport on cb4 was 780 × 493: the \`width\` and \`height\` that \`open\` asks for were not applied by Browser Run. \`logs\` starts again with each document, and a page that loads a new document by itself has no log from before its first script until the next call. A Live View link gives control of the tab to the person who holds it; how long the link works was not measured.`
// %%
md`## One browser, many tabs

\`open\` makes a tab in the shared browser. Each page has its own browser context, thus two pages on one origin do not share cookies, \`localStorage\` or IndexedDB. Two pages opened with the same \`shared: "label"\` share one context. \`open\` with \`own: true\` gives the page a browser of its own, which closes with the page and is paid for separately.

Each call makes a new WebSocket to the browser, attaches to the tab by its target id, and closes the socket. No Durable Object holds a connection. Thus a page keeps its state between calls, and two calls at one time each have a connection.

The tabs share the fate of the browser. If the browser ends, each page is closed, and the next call answers \`PageClosed\` with the reason: \`browser ended\`, \`time ran out\`, \`page ended\`, \`browser stopped answering\`. The tabs also share the memory of the browser. Cloudflare does not publish the limit. Three tabs of the Brain's own page stopped the browser on cb4 two times in two attempts (see Measured). Two tabs did not.

Two calls that start the browser at one time: the row \`starting/shared\` is a lock, and the call that does not get it waits for the browser row. If a start dies after Browser Run made the browser, the tick closes the session that began at the time of that start. The tick does not close a session it cannot tie to a start of its own, because the list of sessions is for the Cloudflare account and holds the sessions of other Workers.

A cron tick each minute closes a browser whose time ran out, sends one command to each other browser (Browser Run closes a browser that gets no command for 180 s), and drops the rows of a browser or a tab that is gone.`
// %%
md`## Who can call

The core decides who can call a method. This service does not decide. It records the caller that the core names in \`by\`, and who started the chain of calls in \`origin\`.

The rule of each method is \`caller.trusted\`: the owner, a token that names the method, or an account with a grant. \`settings\` and \`liveView\` are \`caller.session\`, the owner's own session, and the owner cannot put a different rule for them. A Worker is not \`trusted\`: the owner opens a method to a Worker with \`rule.put\`, and the Worker must name the method in its \`calls\`. A rule can read the seconds:

\`\`\`js
await client.procedure("rule.put", {
  target: "com.lopecode.brain.browser.run",
  allow: 'caller.trusted || (caller.worker == "brain-x-reader" && int(request.params.seconds) <= 30)'
})
\`\`\`

Addresses. \`open\`, \`goto\` and \`run\` refuse an address that is not \`http:\` or \`https:\`, \`localhost\`, a name that ends in \`.localhost\`, \`.internal\` or \`.local\`, and a private or link-local IP address. Only the address in the call is read. A redirect, and what the page loads by itself, are not checked. The browser runs in Cloudflare's network and not in the Brain's.

If a later build signs a page in to the Brain, \`eval\` and \`goto\` on that page must be for the owner's own session only: a caller that can run JavaScript in a signed-in page can do what the page can do.

The assistant has no tool for this service. A page reads text from any address, and that text would come into the assistant's turn.`
// %%
md`## Time and cost

Cloudflare bills Browser Run by the browser-hour: $0.09 an hour, 10 hours a month in the Workers Paid plan, and the total of a month is rounded to the hour ([pricing](https://developers.cloudflare.com/browser-rendering/pricing/), read 2026-10-07). One second is $0.000025. The count is for the account and not for this service.

The life of a browser is bought before it is used. \`paidUntil\` is the time it is bought to. \`open\` and \`run\` buy what is missing for \`seconds\` from now and buy nothing when \`paidUntil\` already covers that. \`extend\` adds seconds. Each purchase answers \`{ paidUntil, bought, costUsd }\`, with \`costUsd = bought × 0.000025\`.

When \`paidUntil\` has passed, the next call or the next tick closes the browser, whatever tabs are open. The use of a page does not add time. A browser with time left and no page stays up, so that calls that come together share one browser. \`close { all: true }\` ends it early, and time that was bought and not used is not given back. The tick comes each minute, thus a browser can live up to 60 s after \`paidUntil\` when no call comes.

The core charges for the time (see **Prices and credits** in \`@tomlarkworthy/brain-core\`). \`open\`, \`run\` and \`extend\` declare a price: the seconds in the query string × 0.000025, with 60 for \`open\` and 30 for \`run\` when the query has no seconds. The core charges that to the account of who started the call before the call reaches this service, and refuses the call with 402 \`OutOfCredits\` when the account's day is spent; no browser starts. The answer carries \`x-brain-cost\`, the \`costUsd\` of what was bought, and the core gives the difference back. Thus a call that finds the time already bought costs 0. A call that is refused or fails is not charged, by the core's rule for an answer that is not 2xx.

This service has no spending limit of its own. Until 2026-10-08 07:27 CEST it had a daily limit of seconds and a ledger; the core's price took their place, so that one mechanism counts money.

Limits. A \`run\` or an \`open\` that starts a browser and then fails (the address does not load) is given back in full, and the browser it started lives for the seconds asked for. \`extend\` with no \`seconds\` is refused by the core with 403, because its price cannot be decided.

| Setting | Default | |
|---|---|---|
| \`maxSeconds\` | 600 | the most one call can buy |
| \`maxSession\` | 3600 | the longest life of one browser |
| \`maxTabs\` | 8 | pages open at one time |

\`status\` gives \`usage\`: the browser-seconds that this service's browsers ran today and this month, as the tick counted them, to the minute. The charges are in the core (\`quota.ledger\`). Browser Run's own record (\`/v1/history\` on the binding) has the exact start and end of each session; this service does not read it.`
// %%
md`## Measured

On cb4, 2026-10-08, 04:53 to 05:24 UTC, HeadlessChrome 128. Browser Run's history gave 411.8 browser-seconds for the day's 10 sessions, the probes included.

\`\`\`
run  https://example.com/  cold browser  1831 ms   warm browser  665 ms, 1075 ms
open https://example.com/  cold browser  2036 ms   warm browser  1100 ms, 1134 ms
open the Brain's page      cold browser  2185 ms, 2408 ms   warm browser  1238, 1261, 1076 ms
\`\`\`

\`open\` and \`run\` answer at the load event. The Brain's page was ready (the runtime up, "Sign in" on the page) 5.5 to 6.0 s after navigation, with about 5000 variables.

JavaScript heap of the Brain's page, not signed in, from \`performance.memory\` in each of two tabs:

\`\`\`
+11 s  151 MB  180 MB
+23 s  472 MB  338 MB
+35 s  397 MB  399 MB
\`\`\`

Three tabs of that page: 63 to 77 MB each at +5.5 s. In both attempts the browser stopped answering 13 to 21 s after the third tab opened, and Browser Run closed the session with \`WorkerError\` 92 s and 115 s after its start. Three tabs of example.com held for 30 s with no fault. The cause was not isolated. Memory is the likely one: two tabs reached about 800 MB of heap.

Held on the binding, with a scratch Worker: \`Target.createBrowserContext\` works, and two contexts on one origin did not see each other's \`localStorage\`. Two Worker requests held a connection to one session at one time. \`DELETE /v1/devtools/browser/<id>\` closes a session. \`GET /v1/sessions\` lists the sessions of the account with no owner.

A fault that was found and designed out: with \`Runtime.evaluate\` left waiting on a promise that did not settle, the request's client gave up after 30 s, each later call answered \`BrowserUnavailable\`, and \`close\` did not close the session. \`eval\` now leaves no command waiting: the promise writes its result to a place in the page, and that place is read each 100 to 500 ms. After the change, three calls that waited on promises at one time answered. A probe of the same case with a second request ended in a Worker error; that probe was not repeated and its cause is not known.

Measured on 2026-10-07 with the spike in \`tools/scratch/browser-run\`: a session lived 150 s with no connection and was reattached by its id; one tab of the Brain's page held for 11 min.`
// %%
md`## Not built

Hosting the Brain's own page in a browser. It needs an identity for the page (a session that is not the owner's own), and the proxy as the page's path to a model.

Also not built: a limit on the hosts a page can load (Browser Run has \`guardrails.allowedDomains\`, set when a session starts, thus for a browser and not for a tab); a viewport size; clicks and keys; downloads; the close of a tab that has no row (a call that dies between making the tab and writing its row leaves the tab until the browser ends); a read of \`/v1/history\` for the exact seconds.`
// %%
browser_service = cloudflare.Service("browser", browserApp, {
  crons: ["* * * * *"],
  scheduled: () => browserOps.tick(),
  methods: {
    // The price of a call is the seconds it asks for, at Browser Run's $0.09 an hour. The core charges it to who started the call.
    // The answer says what was bought (x-brain-cost), and the core gives back the rest.
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.open": { type: "procedure", who: "owner", price: "(has(request.params.seconds) ? double(request.params.seconds) : 60.0) * 0.000025" },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.extend": { type: "procedure", who: "owner", price: "double(request.params.seconds) * 0.000025" },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.run": { type: "procedure", who: "owner", price: "(has(request.params.seconds) ? double(request.params.seconds) : 30.0) * 0.000025" },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.list": { type: "query", who: "owner" },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.status": { type: "query", who: "owner" },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.close": { type: "procedure", who: "owner" },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.goto": { type: "procedure", who: "owner" },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.eval": { type: "procedure", who: "owner" },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.text": { type: "query", who: "owner" },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.screenshot": { type: "query", who: "owner" },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.logs": { type: "query", who: "owner" },
    // A Live View link gives the person who holds it the tab. The limits are the spend. Both for the owner's own session only.
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.liveView": { type: "query", who: "owner", allow: "caller.session", fixed: true },
    "com.lopecode.brain.browser.settings": { type: "procedure", who: "owner", allow: "caller.session", fixed: true }
  }
})
// %%
browser_announce = {
  plugins.add("workers", browser_service, { invalidation });
  return "announced browser_service";
}
// %%
// Seconds unless the name says otherwise. The first three are the defaults of the settings the owner can change.
browserLimits = ({
  maxTabs: 8,
  maxSeconds: 600, // the most one call can buy
  maxSession: 3600, // the longest life of one browser
  openSeconds: 60, // what open buys when it names no number
  runSeconds: 30, // the same for run; also the time a run has
  keepAlive: 180000, // ms. Browser Run closes a browser that gets no command for this long: the stop if the tick does not run
  startWait: 15000, // ms a call waits for a browser that another call is starting
  startGrace: 30, // a start that has not finished after this long is taken to have died
  loadTimeout: 30000, // ms
  evalTimeout: 20000, // ms
  maxTimeout: 120000, // ms, eval and run
  evalBytes: 262144, // the JSON of an eval value
  textChars: 100000,
  shotBytes: 2000000,
  logEntries: 200,
  logChars: 500,
  includedSeconds: 36000, // 10 browser-hours a month are in the Workers Paid plan
  dollarsPerSecond: 0.000025 // $0.09 a browser-hour
})
// %%
// Why a URL is refused, or null. Only the address given is read: a redirect and what the page loads are not checked.
browserRefusal = (text) => {
  let u;
  try {
    u = new URL(String(text));
  } catch (e) {
    return "not a URL";
  }
  if (u.protocol !== "http:" && u.protocol !== "https:") return `${u.protocol} is not http or https`;
  const h = u.hostname.toLowerCase().replace(/^\[|\]$/g, "").replace(/\.$/, "");
  if (h === "localhost" || /\.(localhost|internal|local)$/.test(h)) return `${h} is a local name`;
  const v4 = h.match(/^(\d+)\.(\d+)\.(\d+)\.(\d+)$/);
  if (v4) {
    const [a, b] = [Number(v4[1]), Number(v4[2])];
    if (a === 0 || a === 10 || a === 127 || (a === 169 && b === 254) || (a === 172 && b >= 16 && b <= 31) || (a === 192 && b === 168) || (a === 100 && b >= 64 && b <= 127))
      return `${h} is a private address`;
  }
  if (h.includes(":") && (h === "::1" || h === "::" || /^f[cd]/.test(h) || /^fe[89ab]/.test(h) || /^::ffff:/.test(h))) return `${h} is a private address`;
  return null;
}
// %%
// Runs in each document before its scripts: console calls and uncaught errors go to a list the service reads.
// A script added over CDP goes away with the connection that added it, so each call also runs this in the open document.
browserHook = (entries, chars) =>
  `(() => { if (window.__brainLogs) return true; const L = window.__brainLogs = []; const add = (level, parts) => { try { L.push({ t: Date.now(), level, text: parts.map((p) => { try { return typeof p === "string" ? p : p instanceof Error ? String(p.stack || p) : JSON.stringify(p); } catch (e) { return String(p); } }).join(" ").slice(0, ${chars}) }); if (L.length > ${entries}) L.splice(0, L.length - ${entries}); } catch (e) {} }; for (const k of ["log", "info", "warn", "error", "debug"]) { const was = console[k]; console[k] = function (...a) { add(k, a); return was && was.apply(this, a); }; } addEventListener("error", (e) => add("exception", [e.message + (e.filename ? " at " + e.filename + ":" + e.lineno : "")])); addEventListener("unhandledrejection", (e) => add("rejection", [e.reason])); return true; })()`
// %%
// CDP over one WebSocket. send() answers with the result or throws; an error with `gone` means the socket closed.
browserCdp = (ws) => {
  let id = 0, closed = null;
  const wait = new Map(), watchers = new Set();
  const gone = () => Object.assign(new Error("the browser closed the connection"), { gone: true });
  ws.addEventListener("message", (e) => {
    const m = JSON.parse(typeof e.data === "string" ? e.data : new TextDecoder().decode(e.data));
    if (m.id && wait.has(m.id)) {
      const w = wait.get(m.id);
      wait.delete(m.id);
      clearTimeout(w.timer);
      m.error ? w.no(new Error(m.error.message)) : w.yes(m.result);
    } else if (m.method) for (const w of [...watchers]) w(m);
  });
  ws.addEventListener("close", (e) => {
    closed = `${e.code} ${e.reason}`;
    for (const w of wait.values()) (clearTimeout(w.timer), w.no(gone()));
    wait.clear();
    for (const w of [...watchers]) w(null);
  });
  return {
    isClosed: () => closed !== null,
    closedWhy: () => closed,
    send: (method, params = {}, sessionId, timeout = 10000) =>
      new Promise((yes, no) => {
        if (closed !== null) return no(gone());
        const n = ++id;
        const timer = setTimeout(() => (wait.delete(n), no(Object.assign(new Error(`${method} did not answer in ${timeout} ms`), { status: 504, error: "Timeout" }))), timeout);
        wait.set(n, { yes, no, timer });
        try {
          ws.send(JSON.stringify({ id: n, method, params, ...(sessionId ? { sessionId } : {}) }));
        } catch (e) {
          clearTimeout(timer);
          wait.delete(n);
          no(gone());
        }
      }),
    // true when an event matches before the time is up, false at the time or when the socket closes.
    until: (match, timeout) =>
      new Promise((yes) => {
        const done = (v) => (clearTimeout(timer), watchers.delete(w), yes(v));
        const w = (m) => (m === null ? done(false) : match(m) ? done(true) : null);
        const timer = setTimeout(() => done(false), timeout);
        watchers.add(w);
      }),
    close: () => {
      try {
        ws.close(1000, "done");
      } catch (e) {}
    }
  };
}
// %%
// Rows: browser/<session> one per browser; paid/<session> the time its life is bought to; page/<name> one per tab;
// ended/<name> why a page that is gone closed;
// usage/<UTC day> browser-seconds counted; starting/<what> a start in progress; settings.
browserOps = {
  const L = browserLimits;
  const stop = (status, error, message) => Object.assign(new Error(message), { status, error });
  const sleep = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
  const day = (t) => new Date(t).toISOString().slice(0, 10);
  const vals = async (prefix) => (await rows.list(prefix)).map((r) => r.value);
  const settings = async () => {
    const set = (await rows.get("settings")) || {};
    return { maxTabs: set.maxTabs || L.maxTabs, maxSeconds: set.maxSeconds || L.maxSeconds, maxSession: set.maxSession || L.maxSession };
  };
  const http = async (path, method = "GET") => {
    const r = await browser.fetch(path, { method });
    let body = null;
    try {
      body = await r.json();
    } catch (e) {}
    return { status: r.status, body };
  };
  // Oldest first. The first one that is not a page's own is the shared browser.
  const browsers = async () => (await vals("browser/")).sort((a, b) => a.opened - b.opened || (a.sessionId < b.sessionId ? -1 : 1));
  const pages = () => vals("page/");
  const paidUntil = async (sid) => (await rows.get("paid/" + sid)) || 0;
  // The sessions Browser Run reports for the account, or null when it does not answer. Other Workers' sessions are in the list.
  const sessions = async () => {
    const r = await http("/v1/sessions").catch(() => null);
    return r && r.status === 200 && r.body && Array.isArray(r.body.sessions) ? r.body.sessions : null;
  };
  const alive = async (sid) => {
    const s = await sessions();
    return s ? s.some((x) => x.sessionId === sid) : true;
  };
  // What a call bought. The core charged the price of the call before it came here; costUsd goes back as x-brain-cost.
  const receipt = (until, bought) => ({ paidUntil: until, bought, costUsd: Math.round(bought * L.dollarsPerSecond * 1e6) / 1e6 });
  // Browser time that was used, counted once per browser into the UTC day of the count.
  const meter = async (b, at) => {
    const key = "usage/" + day(at), u = (await rows.get(key)) || { seconds: 0 };
    await rows.put(key, { seconds: u.seconds + Math.max(0, at - b.counted) / 1000 });
    return { ...b, counted: at };
  };
  const tomb = (name, closed, at) => rows.put("ended/" + name, { name, closed, at });
  // A browser is over: its time is counted, its rows go, and each of its pages says why.
  const end = async (sid, reason, at, kill = true) => {
    if (kill) await http("/v1/devtools/browser/" + sid, "DELETE").catch(() => null);
    const b = await rows.get("browser/" + sid);
    if (b) await meter(b, at);
    await rows.delete("browser/" + sid);
    await rows.delete("paid/" + sid);
    const gone = [];
    for (const p of await pages())
      if (p.sessionId === sid) {
        await rows.delete("page/" + p.name);
        await tomb(p.name, reason, at);
        gone.push(p.name);
      }
    return gone;
  };
  // A browser whose bought time has passed is closed, whatever tabs it has. Each call and the tick do this first.
  const expire = async (at) => {
    const out = [];
    for (const b of await browsers()) if ((await paidUntil(b.sessionId)) <= at) (await end(b.sessionId, "time ran out", at), out.push(b.sessionId));
    return out;
  };
  // Buys what is missing for the browser to live `seconds` from now. Nothing is bought when its time already covers that.
  // `add` buys that many seconds after the time it has. Two purchases at one moment can both count from the same time.
  const cover = async (b, seconds, by, origin, add = false) => {
    const at = Date.now(), s = await settings(), paid = await paidUntil(b.sessionId), from = Math.max(paid, at);
    const until = add ? from + seconds * 1000 : at + seconds * 1000;
    if (until <= paid) return receipt(paid, 0);
    if (until > b.opened + s.maxSession * 1000) throw stop(400, "SessionLimit", `one browser lives ${s.maxSession} s at most, and this one has ${Math.max(0, Math.floor((b.opened + s.maxSession * 1000 - from) / 1000))} s left to buy. The setting is maxSession.`);
    const bought = Math.ceil((until - from) / 1000);
    await rows.put("paid/" + b.sessionId, until);
    return receipt(until, bought);
  };
  // Starts a browser with `seconds` of life.
  const acquire = async (own, seconds, by, origin) => {
    const r = await http("/v1/devtools/browser?keep_alive=" + L.keepAlive, "POST").catch((e) => ({ status: 0, body: String(e) }));
    if (r.status !== 200 || !r.body || !r.body.sessionId) throw stop(502, "BrowserUnavailable", `Browser Run did not start a browser: ${r.status} ${JSON.stringify(r.body).slice(0, 200)}`);
    const at = Date.now(), b = { sessionId: r.body.sessionId, opened: at, counted: at, own, by };
    await rows.put("paid/" + b.sessionId, at + seconds * 1000);
    await rows.put("browser/" + b.sessionId, b);
